{"type":"text","timestamp":1790886283476,"sessionID":"ses_f06dcccfaffePrKoUPTBob5ZgJ","part":{"id":"prt_0f923c02f001osTAjw50Hd5op7","messageID":"msg_0f923a2e7001OPTmrzFmk1exmC","sessionID":"ses_f06dcccfaffePrKoUPTBob5ZgJ","type":"text","text":"{\"action\":\"finish\",\"answer\":{\"signing_key_position\":{\"value\":\"last\",\"citations\":[{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/signer.py\",\"quote\":\"        #: The list of secret keys to try for verifying signatures, from\\n        #: oldest to newest. The newest (last) key is used for signing.\"},{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/signer.py\",\"quote\":\"        if secret_key is None:\\n            secret_key = self.secret_keys[-1]\"},{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/signer.py\",\"quote\":\"    def get_signature(self, value: str | bytes) -> bytes:\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"Returns the signature for the given value.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n        value = want_bytes(value)\\n        key = self.derive_key()\"}]},\"primary_verification_key_order\":{\"value\":\"newest-first\",\"citations\":[{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/signer.py\",\"quote\":\"        for secret_key in reversed(self.secret_keys):\\n            key = self.derive_key(secret_key)\\n\\n            if self.algorithm.verify_signature(key, value, sig):\\n                return True\"},{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/signer.py\",\"quote\":\"        #: The list of secret keys to try for verifying signatures, from\\n        #: oldest to newest. The newest (last) key is used for signing.\"}]},\"fallback_key_order\":{\"value\":\"oldest-first\",\"citations\":[{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/serializer.py\",\"quote\":\"            for secret_key in self.secret_keys:\\n                yield fallback(secret_key, salt=salt, **kwargs)\"},{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/serializer.py\",\"quote\":\"        #: The list of secret keys to try for verifying signatures, from\\n        #: oldest to newest. The newest (last) key is used for signing.\\n        #:\\n        #: This allows a key rotation system to keep a list of allowed\\n        #: keys and remove expired ones.\\n        self.secret_keys: list[bytes] = _make_keys_list(secret_key)\"}]},\"primary_before_fallback\":{\"value\":true,\"citations\":[{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/serializer.py\",\"quote\":\"        yield self.make_signer(salt)\\n\\n        for fallback in self.fallback_signers:\"},{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/serializer.py\",\"quote\":\"    def iter_unsigners(self, salt: str | bytes | None = None) -> cabc.Iterator[Signer]:\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"Iterates over all signers to be tried for unsigning. Starts\\n        with the configured signer, then constructs each signer\\n        specified in ``fallback_signers``.\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"\"},{\"path\":\"src/itsdangerous/serializer.py\",\"quote\":\"        for signer in self.iter_unsigners(salt):\\n            try:\\n                return self.load_payload(signer.unsign(s))\\n            except BadSignature as err:\\n                last_exception = err\"}]},\"notes\":\"Repository is itsdangerous. With a secret-key list ordered oldest to newest: (1) the default Signer signs with the last (newest) key, since get_signature calls derive_key() which defaults to self.secret_keys[-1]; (2) the primary signer verifies newest-first, iterating reversed(self.secret_keys); (3) Serializer.iter_unsigners constructs one fallback signer per individual key, iterating self.secret_keys in stored order, i.e. oldest-first, each fallback receiving a single key; (4) the primary signer (make_signer, which gets the full key list) is yielded before any fallback signer, and loads tries them in iteration order. Note that each primary verification attempt already walks all keys newest-first before the fallbacks are consulted. Default Serializer uses json serializer, Signer class, and an empty default_fallback_signers list, so fallbacks only exist if supplied. This is a source explanation, not a formal proof.\"}","time":{"start":1790886264879,"end":1790886283462}}}
